<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristeidis Stergiadis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristeidis Stergiadis (1861–1949) was a Greek lawyer and administrator, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> as High Commissioner of the Smyrna zone from 1919 to 1922. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, he was known for his stern efforts to treat Muslims and Christians impartially, which made him unpopular with many local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. He left Smyrna as Turkish forces approached in September 1922 and spent the rest of his life in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristeidis-stergiadis.jpg" alt="Aristeidis Stergiadis" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stergiadis,_Paraskevopoulos_and_Pangalos,_Smyrna_October_1920.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
